IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `clwa-dates`

-- Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT), chapter 5 (personal injuries claims -- pre-court procedures) -- the questions of the
-- person bringing a personal injury claim.
--
-- SEAT: the claimant: the injured person (or, for a fatal injury, the dead person's dependants or estate; for a child, a
-- parent or legal guardian giving notice for the child) who means to sue someone in the ACT for damages for personal
-- injury. "Does the pre-court procedure apply to me?", "By when must I give notice of my claim?", "Is my notice late, and
-- what must I add?", "Is the respondent taken to accept my notice?", "By when must the respondent answer me, or I answer
-- it?", "Must I go to the respondent's doctor?", "Can I go to court yet?". The respondent, its insurer and the court get no
-- seat: what the respondent did and what the court decides are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a number, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. Three duties with deadlines are regulative rules run inside questions: the claimant's notice of claim (s 51 (3),
-- (4), (7)), the respondent's response (s 54 (2), (3)), and the respondent's duty to try to resolve the claim followed
-- by the claimant's answer to its offer (s 61 (1), (4)).
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: ss 49-52, 54, 55 (3) (b), 59-62 (except s 56 multiple respondents, s 57-58 contributors), 64,
-- 66-68, 71, 79. Machinery and duties between respondents and contributors are out: NOTES.md.
--
-- The Civil Law (Wrongs) Regulation 2003 is NOT deposited. Chapter 5 lets it prescribe the information and documents a
-- notice needs (s 51 (2)), reasonable excuses (s 51 (8)) and the periods for many steps; where the Act gives a period
-- "if no period is prescribed", this encoding uses the Act's period (finding 3). A prescribed period would change the
-- answers.
--
-- Edition: Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT), A2002-40, Republication No 77 (R77), effective 16 May 2026, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A2002-40.txt.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court or the respondent are INPUTS. Answers
-- follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)";
-- an answer where the text is limited by its own wording, or silent, is marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002, chapter 5: the questions of the person bringing a personal injury claim`

§§ `s 50 -- Application of chapter 5`

-- s 49: "claim means a claim (however described) for damages based on a liability for personal injury ... and, for a
-- fatal injury, includes a claim for the dead person's dependants or estate."
-- s 50 (1): chapter 5 applies to all claims for damages for personal injury, including motor accident claims under the
-- Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019, ch 5. (2): not to a claim for compensation under the Workers Compensation Act 1951,
-- or a claim where both Limitation Act 1985 s 30A notices (injuries to children) have been given. (3): not where a
-- workers compensation claim has been made for the injury. (4): despite (3), part 5.2 applies if the claim is a motor
-- accident claim, or is made against someone who is not an employer or insurer under the Workers Compensation Act,
-- chapter 6, even if also made against one.
DECLARE `Does chapter 5 apply to my claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: chapter 5 (the pre-court procedures) applies to your claim (s 50 (1))`
    `only part 5.2 (claims procedures) applies: a workers compensation claim was made for the injury, but s 50 (4) keeps part 5.2; the rest of chapter 5 does not apply (the text's no, s 50 (3))`
    `no: chapter 5 does not apply to a claim for workers compensation itself (the text's no, s 50 (2) (a))`
    `no: chapter 5 does not apply where both Limitation Act 1985 s 30A notices have been given (the text's no, s 50 (2) (b))`
    `no: a workers compensation claim was made for the injury (the text's no, s 50 (3))`
    `chapter 5 is about claims for damages for personal injury; this is not one, and the Act says nothing more about it here (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Does the pre-court procedure of the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT), chapter 5, apply to my claim (s 50)? It applies to claims for damages for personal injury (including motor accident common law claims), but not to workers compensation claims, and only in part where a workers compensation claim was made for the injury.
GIVEN `damages for personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for damages for a personal injury (bodily injury, mental or nervous shock, or a death), on any legal basis (negligence, contract, breach of statutory duty)
      `a workers compensation claim itself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this claim is itself a claim for compensation under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
      `both s 30A notices given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, for an injury to a child, notice has been given for you under the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 30A (2) and the respondent has given notice under s 30A (6)
      `workers compensation claimed for the injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a claim for workers compensation under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT) has been made for this injury
      `a motor accident claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a motor accident claim to which the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019 (ACT), chapter 5 (common law damages) applies
      `against someone other than employer or insurer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is made against someone who is not your employer or its workers compensation insurer for the injury (even if it is also made against them)
GIVETH A `Does chapter 5 apply to my claim`
`s 50 -- does chapter 5 apply to my claim` `damages for personal injury` `a workers compensation claim itself` `both s 30A notices given` `workers compensation claimed for the injury` `a motor accident claim` `against someone other than employer or insurer` MEANS
    IF `a workers compensation claim itself`
    THEN `no: chapter 5 does not apply to a claim for workers compensation itself (the text's no, s 50 (2) (a))`
    ELSE IF NOT `damages for personal injury`
         THEN `chapter 5 is about claims for damages for personal injury; this is not one, and the Act says nothing more about it here (NOTHING-kind)`
         ELSE IF `both s 30A notices given`
              THEN `no: chapter 5 does not apply where both Limitation Act 1985 s 30A notices have been given (the text's no, s 50 (2) (b))`
              ELSE IF NOT `workers compensation claimed for the injury`
                   THEN `yes: chapter 5 (the pre-court procedures) applies to your claim (s 50 (1))`
                   ELSE IF `a motor accident claim` OR `against someone other than employer or insurer`
                        THEN `only part 5.2 (claims procedures) applies: a workers compensation claim was made for the injury, but s 50 (4) keeps part 5.2; the rest of chapter 5 does not apply (the text's no, s 50 (3))`
                        ELSE `no: a workers compensation claim was made for the injury (the text's no, s 50 (3))`

§§ `s 51 and s 60 -- Notice of claim, and legal disability`

-- s 51 (3): for a proceeding not based on a motor accident claim or child abuse claim, notice must be given within the
-- period ending on the earlier of (a) the day 9 months after the accident (or, if symptoms are not immediately apparent,
-- after symptoms first appear) and (b) the day 4 months after the later of the day the claimant first instructs a lawyer
-- to advise about seeking damages and the day the respondent is identified.
-- s 51 (4): for a motor accident claim, within 3 months after the latest of seven days tied to the Motor Accident
-- Injuries Act 2019 (WPI of 10% taken or assessed, notices under its ss 141 (5), 157 (2), 164 (2), 213 (4), ACAT
-- orders). Which applies is the claimant's fact: the claimant states the latest of them.
-- s 51 (5): for a child abuse claim, within a reasonable time before the claimant brings the proceeding. No fixed day.
-- s 60: a claimant need not comply while under a legal disability (as under the Limitation Act 1985); the period begins
-- when the disability ends, and the accident day is read as the day the disability ends. Fork F4: every day a period
-- counts from is moved to no earlier than the day the disability ended.
DECLARE `The kind of claim` IS ONE OF
    `a personal injury claim that is not a motor accident claim or a child abuse claim`
    `a motor accident claim`
    `a child abuse claim`

-- s 60 (3) (a), (2), (3) (b): a day a period counts from, moved to the day the legal disability ended if earlier.
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `under a legal disability` IS A BOOLEAN, `disability ended on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the counting day` `the day` `under a legal disability` `disability ended on` MEANS
    IF `under a legal disability`
    THEN `the later of` `the day` `and` `disability ended on`
    ELSE `the day`

-- s 51 (3) (a) and (b), as moved by s 60. Without a lawyer yet instructed, limb (b) has not begun: the period ends on
-- limb (a)'s day, as things stand.
GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE, `symptoms immediately apparent` IS A BOOLEAN, `symptoms first appeared on` IS A DATE,
      `instructed a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN, `first instructed a lawyer on` IS A DATE, `respondent identified on` IS A DATE,
      `under a legal disability` IS A BOOLEAN, `disability ended on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 51 (3) last day` `the accident day` `symptoms immediately apparent` `symptoms first appeared on` `instructed a lawyer` `first instructed a lawyer on` `respondent identified on` `under a legal disability` `disability ended on` MEANS
    IF `instructed a lawyer`
    THEN `the earlier of` `limb (a)` `and` `limb (b)`
    ELSE `limb (a)`
    WHERE
        `the day limb (a) counts from` MEANS
            IF `symptoms immediately apparent`
            THEN `the accident day`
            ELSE `symptoms first appeared on`
        `limb (a)` MEANS `the day` 9 `months after` (`the counting day` `the day limb (a) counts from` `under a legal disability` `disability ended on`)
        `limb (b)` MEANS `the day` 4 `months after` (`the counting day` (`the later of` `first instructed a lawyer on` `and` `respondent identified on`) `under a legal disability` `disability ended on`)

DECLARE `The time limit for my notice of claim` IS ONE OF
    `a fixed last day: the earlier of 9 months after the accident (or the first symptoms) and 4 months after you instructed a lawyer or identified the respondent, whichever is later (s 51 (3))`
    `a fixed last day: 3 months after the latest of the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019 days in s 51 (4)`
    `no fixed day: a child abuse claim needs notice within a reasonable time before you begin the proceeding (s 51 (5))`
    `not yet: you are under a legal disability, and need not give notice while it lasts; the period begins when it ends (s 60)`

@export What time limit applies to my notice of claim to the person I mean to sue for personal injury (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 51 (3)-(5), s 60)? Before suing you must give each respondent written notice of the claim.
GIVEN `the kind of claim` IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of personal injury claim yours is: a motor accident claim (injury caused by or arising out of the use of a motor vehicle), a child abuse claim (injury from physical or sexual abuse suffered as a child), or another personal injury claim
      `under a legal disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been under a legal disability (as under the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT): for example, a child, or a person unable to manage their affairs because of a mental disability) since the accident
      `disability has ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, whether that legal disability has now ended (for example, you have turned 18)
GIVETH A `The time limit for my notice of claim`
`s 51 -- what time limit applies to my notice of claim` `the kind of claim` `under a legal disability` `disability has ended` MEANS
    IF `under a legal disability` AND NOT `disability has ended`
    THEN `not yet: you are under a legal disability, and need not give notice while it lasts; the period begins when it ends (s 60)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the kind of claim`
         WHEN `a personal injury claim that is not a motor accident claim or a child abuse claim` THEN `a fixed last day: the earlier of 9 months after the accident (or the first symptoms) and 4 months after you instructed a lawyer or identified the respondent, whichever is later (s 51 (3))`
         WHEN `a motor accident claim` THEN `a fixed last day: 3 months after the latest of the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019 days in s 51 (4)`
         WHEN `a child abuse claim` THEN `no fixed day: a child abuse claim needs notice within a reasonable time before you begin the proceeding (s 51 (5))`

-- The last day, where there is one (NOTHING for a child abuse claim, and while a legal disability lasts).
GIVEN `the kind of claim` IS A `The kind of claim`, `the accident day` IS A DATE, `symptoms immediately apparent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `symptoms first appeared on` IS A DATE, `instructed a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN, `first instructed a lawyer on` IS A DATE,
      `respondent identified on` IS A DATE, `the latest s 51 (4) day` IS A DATE, `under a legal disability` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `disability has ended` IS A BOOLEAN, `disability ended on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the last day for the notice` `the kind of claim` `the accident day` `symptoms immediately apparent` `symptoms first appeared on` `instructed a lawyer` `first instructed a lawyer on` `respondent identified on` `the latest s 51 (4) day` `under a legal disability` `disability has ended` `disability ended on` MEANS
    IF `under a legal disability` AND NOT `disability has ended`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `the kind of claim`
         WHEN `a personal injury claim that is not a motor accident claim or a child abuse claim` THEN JUST (`the s 51 (3) last day` `the accident day` `symptoms immediately apparent` `symptoms first appeared on` `instructed a lawyer` `first instructed a lawyer on` `respondent identified on` `under a legal disability` `disability ended on`)
         WHEN `a motor accident claim` THEN JUST (`the day` 3 `months after` (`the counting day` `the latest s 51 (4) day` `under a legal disability` `disability ended on`))
         WHEN `a child abuse claim` THEN NOTHING

@export What is the last day for giving notice of my personal injury claim (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 51 (3), (4), s 60)? Answers no date for a child abuse claim (notice within a reasonable time before suing) or while you are under a legal disability.
GIVEN `the kind of claim` IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of personal injury claim yours is: a motor accident claim, a child abuse claim, or another personal injury claim
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident (the incident out of which the injury arose)
      `symptoms immediately apparent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether symptoms of the injury were immediately apparent at the accident
      `symptoms first appeared on` IS A DATE @desc If they were not, the day symptoms first appeared (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `instructed a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have instructed a lawyer to advise you about seeking damages for the injury
      `first instructed a lawyer on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day you first did (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `respondent identified on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person you mean to sue (the respondent) was identified
      `the latest s 51 (4) day` IS A DATE @desc For a motor accident claim only: the latest of the days in s 51 (4) that apply to you (for example, the due date stated in a Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019 s 157 (2) notice, or the day you were told you are taken to have a WPI of 10%); otherwise any date
      `under a legal disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been under a legal disability (for example, a child, or unable to manage your affairs) since the accident
      `disability has ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, whether it has now ended
      `disability ended on` IS A DATE @desc If it has ended, the day it ended (otherwise any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 51 -- the last day for my notice of claim` `the kind of claim` `the accident day` `symptoms immediately apparent` `symptoms first appeared on` `instructed a lawyer` `first instructed a lawyer on` `respondent identified on` `the latest s 51 (4) day` `under a legal disability` `disability has ended` `disability ended on` MEANS
    `the last day for the notice` `the kind of claim` `the accident day` `symptoms immediately apparent` `symptoms first appeared on` `instructed a lawyer` `first instructed a lawyer on` `respondent identified on` `the latest s 51 (4) day` `under a legal disability` `disability has ended` `disability ended on`

§§ `The duties with deadlines (regulative rules)`

-- The parties and their steps under chapter 5.
DECLARE `Pre-court party` IS ONE OF `the claimant`, `the respondent`

DECLARE `Pre-court step` IS ONE OF
    `give notice of claim`
    `give a reasonable excuse for the delay`
    `give a response to the notice of claim`
    `make a written offer of settlement or settle the claim`
    `respond in writing to the offer`

-- s 51 (1), (3), (4), (7): the claimant must give notice by the last day. If it is not given in time, the duty continues
-- (Legislation Act 2001 s 152, as the Act's notes say), and for the s 51 (3) period a reasonable excuse for the delay
-- must be given, in the notice or by separate written notice (s 51 (7)). s 51 (7) speaks only of the s 51 (3) period:
-- for a motor accident claim (s 51 (4)) no excuse is required by the Act (finding 4). Time is counted in day serials, so
-- the rule's deadline is the last day's serial and events are at their days' serials.
GIVEN `the last day serial` IS A NUMBER, `excuse required` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Pre-court party` `Pre-court step`
`give notice of claim by the last day` `the last day serial` `excuse required` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `give notice of claim`
    WITHIN `the last day serial`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST `give notice of claim late` `excuse required`

GIVEN `excuse required` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Pre-court party` `Pre-court step`
`give notice of claim late` `excuse required` MEANS
    IF `excuse required`
    THEN PARTY `the claimant`
         MUST `give notice of claim`
         HENCE (PARTY `the claimant` MUST `give a reasonable excuse for the delay`)
    ELSE PARTY `the claimant`
         MUST `give notice of claim`

-- Runs the notice rule over what happened: TRUE where the claimant's s 51 duties are met on these facts.
GIVEN `the last day` IS A DATE, `excuse required` IS A BOOLEAN, `notice given on` IS A DATE, `excuse given` IS A BOOLEAN, `excuse given on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 51 duties are met` `the last day` `excuse required` `notice given on` `excuse given` `excuse given on` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`give notice of claim by the last day` (DATE_SERIAL `the last day`) `excuse required`) 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHERE
        `the notice serial` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `notice given on`
        `the excuse serial` MEANS DATE_SERIAL (`the later of` `excuse given on` `and` `notice given on`)
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `excuse given`
            THEN LIST (WAITUNTIL `the notice serial`), (EVENT `the claimant` `give notice of claim` `the notice serial`), (EVENT `the claimant` `give a reasonable excuse for the delay` `the excuse serial`)
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL `the notice serial`), (EVENT `the claimant` `give notice of claim` `the notice serial`)

§§ `s 51 -- Have I given notice in time`

DECLARE `My notice of claim` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you gave notice on or before the last day (s 51 (3) or (4))`
    `late, but you have given a reasonable excuse for the delay (s 51 (7)): the s 51 duty is met`
    `late, and you still owe a reasonable excuse for the delay, in the notice or by separate written notice (s 51 (7)); until it is given the notice is not given as required, and you cannot proceed unless the respondent or the court lets you (s 59)`
    `late: s 51 (7) asks for an excuse only when the s 51 (3) period is missed, and says nothing for a motor accident claim (NOTHING-kind); the notice is not given within the s 51 (4) period`
    `not yet given, and the last day has not passed`
    `not yet given, and the last day has passed: you must still give it (the duty continues, Legislation Act 2001 s 152), with a reasonable excuse for the delay unless it is a motor accident claim (s 51 (7))`
    `no fixed last day: a child abuse claim needs notice within a reasonable time before you begin the proceeding (s 51 (5))`
    `not yet required: you are under a legal disability, and the period begins when it ends (s 60)`

@export Have I given notice of my personal injury claim in time, and if not, what must I still do (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 51, s 60)? A late notice needs a reasonable excuse for the delay (except, on the Act's words, for a motor accident claim).
GIVEN `the kind of claim` IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of personal injury claim yours is: a motor accident claim, a child abuse claim, or another personal injury claim
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident (the incident out of which the injury arose)
      `symptoms immediately apparent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether symptoms of the injury were immediately apparent at the accident
      `symptoms first appeared on` IS A DATE @desc If they were not, the day symptoms first appeared (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `instructed a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have instructed a lawyer to advise you about seeking damages for the injury
      `first instructed a lawyer on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day you first did (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `respondent identified on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person you mean to sue (the respondent) was identified
      `the latest s 51 (4) day` IS A DATE @desc For a motor accident claim only: the latest of the days in s 51 (4) that apply to you; otherwise any date
      `under a legal disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been under a legal disability (for example, a child, or unable to manage your affairs) since the accident
      `disability has ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, whether it has now ended
      `disability ended on` IS A DATE @desc If it has ended, the day it ended (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the respondent written notice of the claim
      `notice given on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day you gave it (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `excuse given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the respondent a reasonable excuse for any delay, in the notice or by separate written notice
      `excuse given on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day you gave it (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `My notice of claim`
`s 51 -- have I given my notice of claim in time` `the kind of claim` `the accident day` `symptoms immediately apparent` `symptoms first appeared on` `instructed a lawyer` `first instructed a lawyer on` `respondent identified on` `the latest s 51 (4) day` `under a legal disability` `disability has ended` `disability ended on` `notice given` `notice given on` `excuse given` `excuse given on` `today` MEANS
    IF `under a legal disability` AND NOT `disability has ended`
    THEN `not yet required: you are under a legal disability, and the period begins when it ends (s 60)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the last day for the notice` `the kind of claim` `the accident day` `symptoms immediately apparent` `symptoms first appeared on` `instructed a lawyer` `first instructed a lawyer on` `respondent identified on` `the latest s 51 (4) day` `under a legal disability` `disability has ended` `disability ended on`
         WHEN NOTHING THEN `no fixed last day: a child abuse claim needs notice within a reasonable time before you begin the proceeding (s 51 (5))`
         WHEN JUST `the last day` THEN
            IF NOT `notice given`
            THEN IF `the day` `today` `is after` `the last day`
                 THEN `not yet given, and the last day has passed: you must still give it (the duty continues, Legislation Act 2001 s 152), with a reasonable excuse for the delay unless it is a motor accident claim (s 51 (7))`
                 ELSE `not yet given, and the last day has not passed`
            ELSE IF `the day` `notice given on` `is on or before` `the last day`
                 THEN `in time: you gave notice on or before the last day (s 51 (3) or (4))`
                 ELSE IF NOT `excuse required`
                      THEN `late: s 51 (7) asks for an excuse only when the s 51 (3) period is missed, and says nothing for a motor accident claim (NOTHING-kind); the notice is not given within the s 51 (4) period`
                      ELSE IF `the s 51 duties are met` `the last day` `excuse required` `notice given on` `excuse given` `excuse given on`
                           THEN `late, but you have given a reasonable excuse for the delay (s 51 (7)): the s 51 duty is met`
                           ELSE `late, and you still owe a reasonable excuse for the delay, in the notice or by separate written notice (s 51 (7)); until it is given the notice is not given as required, and you cannot proceed unless the respondent or the court lets you (s 59)`
    WHERE
        `excuse required` MEANS `the kind of claim` EQUALS `a personal injury claim that is not a motor accident claim or a child abuse claim`

§§ `s 52 and s 54 -- The respondent's response, and the presumption that my notice complies`

-- s 54 (2): within the required period the respondent must give the claimant a written response stating whether it is
-- satisfied the notice is a complying notice of claim; if not, identifying the noncompliance and whether it waives it;
-- and if it does not waive, allowing a reasonable period of at least 1 month to satisfy it or remedy the noncompliance.
-- s 54 (3): "If the respondent does not give a response within the required period, the respondent is conclusively
-- presumed to be satisfied that the notice is a complying notice of claim."
-- s 54 (5) and s 52 (6) (no period prescribed): 1 month after the respondent received the notice (responding directly,
-- s 52 (1)); 1 month after it is given further information it asked for (s 52 (3) (a)); 1 month after the claimant tells
-- it the claimant considers it properly a respondent (s 52 (2) (b), (4) (b)). Fork F5: one route is asked; where both
-- (A) and (B) of s 54 (5) (b) (ii) apply the later day would govern.
-- Fork F6: a respondent that says nothing at all within the s 52 (1) month is treated as not giving a response within
-- the required period, so the presumption applies.
DECLARE `How the respondent's period runs` IS ONE OF
    `from the day it received my notice of claim (s 52 (1))`
    `from the day I gave it the further information it asked for (s 52 (2) (a), (3))`
    `from the day I told it I consider it properly a respondent (s 52 (2) (b) or (4) (b))`

DECLARE `What the respondent's response said` IS ONE OF
    `that it is satisfied my notice is a complying notice of claim`
    `that it is not satisfied, but waives the noncompliance`
    `that it is not satisfied and does not waive, allowing me a period to fix it`

GIVEN `the respondent's last day serial` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Pre-court party` `Pre-court step`
`the respondent must give a response within the required period` `the respondent's last day serial` MEANS
    PARTY `the respondent`
    MUST `give a response to the notice of claim`
    WITHIN `the respondent's last day serial`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the respondent` BECAUSE "no response within the required period: the respondent is conclusively presumed to be satisfied that the notice is a complying notice of claim (s 54 (3))"

DECLARE `Is my notice taken to comply` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the respondent gave no response within the required period, so it is conclusively presumed to be satisfied that your notice is a complying notice of claim (s 54 (3))`
    `yes: the respondent's response says it is satisfied that your notice is a complying notice of claim (s 54 (2) (a))`
    `the respondent waived the noncompliance (s 54 (2) (b)): you may proceed with the claim (s 59 (1) (b))`
    `not yet: the respondent is not satisfied and does not waive; it must allow you a reasonable period of at least 1 month to satisfy it or fix the noncompliance (s 54 (2) (c))`
    `not yet decided: the respondent's required period has not ended and it has not responded`

@export Is the respondent taken to be satisfied that my notice of claim complies (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 54)? If the respondent gives no response within the required period (1 month, unless a regulation sets another), it is conclusively presumed to be satisfied.
GIVEN `the route` IS A `How the respondent's period runs` @desc Which step the respondent's 1-month period runs from: receiving your notice; your giving it further information it asked for; or your telling it you consider it properly a respondent
      `the period runs from` IS A DATE @desc The day of that step
      `responded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent has given you a written response saying whether it is satisfied that your notice complies
      `responded on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it did (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `what it said` IS A `What the respondent's response said` @desc If it responded, what the response said (otherwise any choice; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Is my notice taken to comply`
`s 54 -- is the respondent taken to be satisfied that my notice complies` `the route` `the period runs from` `responded` `responded on` `what it said` `today` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the respondent must give a response within the required period` (DATE_SERIAL `the respondent's last day`)) 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN
        CONSIDER `what it said`
        WHEN `that it is satisfied my notice is a complying notice of claim` THEN `yes: the respondent's response says it is satisfied that your notice is a complying notice of claim (s 54 (2) (a))`
        WHEN `that it is not satisfied, but waives the noncompliance` THEN `the respondent waived the noncompliance (s 54 (2) (b)): you may proceed with the claim (s 59 (1) (b))`
        WHEN `that it is not satisfied and does not waive, allowing me a period to fix it` THEN `not yet: the respondent is not satisfied and does not waive; it must allow you a reasonable period of at least 1 month to satisfy it or fix the noncompliance (s 54 (2) (c))`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `the day` `the last day looked at` `is after` `the respondent's last day`
        THEN `yes: the respondent gave no response within the required period, so it is conclusively presumed to be satisfied that your notice is a complying notice of claim (s 54 (3))`
        ELSE `not yet decided: the respondent's required period has not ended and it has not responded`
    WHERE
        `the respondent's last day` MEANS `the day` 1 `months after` `the period runs from`
        `the last day looked at` MEANS
            IF `responded`
            THEN `responded on`
            ELSE `today`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `responded`
            THEN LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `responded on`)), (EVENT `the respondent` `give a response to the notice of claim` (DATE_SERIAL `responded on`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `today`))

-- s 54 (2) (c): "allowing the claimant a reasonable period, of at least 1 month, stated in the response". Fewer than 1
-- month is the text's no; whether a period of 1 month or more is reasonable is a judgment, asked as a fact.
DECLARE `The period I was given to fix my notice` IS ONE OF
    `long enough: at least 1 month, and reasonable (s 54 (2) (c))`
    `at least 1 month, but not reasonable: not what s 54 (2) (c) requires (the text's no)`
    `too short: less than 1 month, which s 54 (2) (c) does not allow (the text's no)`

@export Is the period the respondent gave me to fix my notice of claim long enough (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 54 (2) (c))? It must be a reasonable period of at least 1 month, stated in the response.
GIVEN `response given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the respondent gave you its response
      `period ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period stated in the response for you to satisfy the respondent or fix the noncompliance
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the period is reasonable for the action it asks of you (a judgment; answer as you or a court would see it)
GIVETH A `The period I was given to fix my notice`
`s 54 (2) (c) -- is the period to fix my notice long enough` `response given on` `period ends on` `reasonable` MEANS
    IF `the day` `period ends on` `is on or before` (`the date` (0 MINUS 1) `days after` (`the day` 1 `months after` `response given on`))
    THEN `too short: less than 1 month, which s 54 (2) (c) does not allow (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `reasonable`
         THEN `long enough: at least 1 month, and reasonable (s 54 (2) (c))`
         ELSE `at least 1 month, but not reasonable: not what s 54 (2) (c) requires (the text's no)`

§§ `s 59, s 62 and s 79 -- Going further with the claim`

-- s 59 (1): "If a claimant does not give a complying notice of claim, the claimant cannot proceed further with the claim
-- unless" (a) the respondent has given written notice it is satisfied (or that the noncompliance was remedied), or is
-- presumed satisfied under s 54 (3); (b) the respondent has waived any noncompliance; or (c) the court declares the
-- noncompliance remedied or authorises the claimant to proceed despite it.
DECLARE `Can I proceed further with my claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you gave a complying notice of claim`
    `yes: the respondent has said in writing that it is satisfied, or that you have remedied the noncompliance (s 59 (1) (a) (i))`
    `yes: the respondent is presumed to be satisfied that your notice complies (s 59 (1) (a) (ii), s 54 (3))`
    `yes: the respondent waived the noncompliance (s 59 (1) (b))`
    `yes: the court declared the noncompliance remedied, or authorised you to proceed (s 59 (1) (c)), on any conditions it set (s 59 (2))`
    `no: without a complying notice, and none of the s 59 (1) exceptions, you cannot proceed further with the claim (the text's no)`

@export Can I proceed further with my personal injury claim (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 59)? Without a complying notice of claim you cannot, unless the respondent is satisfied (or presumed satisfied), waives the noncompliance, or the court lets you.
GIVEN `complying notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave a notice of claim that complies with chapter 5 (the right content, documents and authority, given in time or with a reasonable excuse for the delay)
      `respondent said satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent has told you in writing that it is satisfied your notice complies, or that you have taken reasonable action to fix it
      `respondent presumed satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent gave no response within its required period, so it is presumed satisfied (s 54 (3))
      `respondent waived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent has waived the noncompliance
      `court let me proceed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court, on your application, has declared that you remedied the noncompliance or authorised you to proceed despite it
GIVETH A `Can I proceed further with my claim`
`s 59 -- can I proceed further with my claim` `complying notice given` `respondent said satisfied` `respondent presumed satisfied` `respondent waived` `court let me proceed` MEANS
    IF `complying notice given`
    THEN `yes: you gave a complying notice of claim`
    ELSE IF `respondent said satisfied`
         THEN `yes: the respondent has said in writing that it is satisfied, or that you have remedied the noncompliance (s 59 (1) (a) (i))`
         ELSE IF `respondent presumed satisfied`
              THEN `yes: the respondent is presumed to be satisfied that your notice complies (s 59 (1) (a) (ii), s 54 (3))`
              ELSE IF `respondent waived`
                   THEN `yes: the respondent waived the noncompliance (s 59 (1) (b))`
                   ELSE IF `court let me proceed`
                        THEN `yes: the court declared the noncompliance remedied, or authorised you to proceed (s 59 (1) (c)), on any conditions it set (s 59 (2))`
                        ELSE `no: without a complying notice, and none of the s 59 (1) exceptions, you cannot proceed further with the claim (the text's no)`

-- s 62 (1): if the claimant does not comply with part 5.2, the court (a) may award the respondent its costs caused by the
-- noncompliance, on the respondent's application; and (b) "may only award interest in the claimant's favour for a period
-- for which the claimant was in noncompliance if the court is satisfied there is a reasonable excuse". (2): the same costs
-- the other way when the respondent does not comply.
DECLARE `Interest for a period I was not complying` IS ONE OF
    `you were complying with part 5.2 in that period: s 62 (1) (b) does not limit interest for it`
    `interest may be awarded for that period: the court is satisfied you had a reasonable excuse (s 62 (1) (b))`
    `no interest for that period: the court may award it only if satisfied you had a reasonable excuse, and it is not (the text's no, s 62 (1) (b))`

@export Can the court award me interest for a period in which I was not complying with the claims procedure (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 62 (1) (b))? Only if the court is satisfied there was a reasonable excuse for the noncompliance.
GIVEN `not complying in that period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the period you ask interest for, you were not complying with the requirements of part 5.2 (for example, your notice of claim was overdue)
      `court satisfied of a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that there is a reasonable excuse for the noncompliance (a judgment of the court)
GIVETH A `Interest for a period I was not complying`
`s 62 -- can I get interest for a period I was not complying` `not complying in that period` `court satisfied of a reasonable excuse` MEANS
    IF NOT `not complying in that period`
    THEN `you were complying with part 5.2 in that period: s 62 (1) (b) does not limit interest for it`
    ELSE IF `court satisfied of a reasonable excuse`
         THEN `interest may be awarded for that period: the court is satisfied you had a reasonable excuse (s 62 (1) (b))`
         ELSE `no interest for that period: the court may award it only if satisfied you had a reasonable excuse, and it is not (the text's no, s 62 (1) (b))`

-- s 79: the court may give leave to begin a proceeding despite noncompliance with chapter 5 if satisfied of an urgent
-- need; the proceeding is then stayed until the claimant complies (or it ends), unless the court is satisfied the
-- claimant has a terminal condition and the trial should be expedited, and orders priority for a trial date, in which
-- case chapter 5 (other than s 79) no longer applies to the injury.
DECLARE `Can I begin a proceeding now` IS ONE OF
    `yes, with the court's leave for an urgent need; the proceeding is stayed until you comply with chapter 5 (or it ends) (s 79 (1), (3))`
    `yes, with the court's leave, and it is not stayed: you have a terminal condition, the trial is to be expedited and given priority; chapter 5 (other than s 79) no longer applies to the injury (s 79 (4), (5))`
    `not under s 79: the court has not given leave for an urgent need; you must first comply with chapter 5 (the text's no)`

@export Can I begin court proceedings for my personal injury now, before finishing the pre-court procedure (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 79)? Only with the court's leave for an urgent need; the proceeding is then stayed until you comply, unless you have a terminal condition and the court gives the trial priority.
GIVEN `leave for an urgent need` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court, on your application, has given you leave to begin the proceeding because it is satisfied there is an urgent need (a judgment of the court)
      `terminal condition, trial expedited` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that you are suffering from a terminal condition and that the trial should be expedited
      `priority ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has ordered that the proceeding be given priority in the allocation of a trial date
GIVETH A `Can I begin a proceeding now`
`s 79 -- can I begin a proceeding now` `leave for an urgent need` `terminal condition, trial expedited` `priority ordered` MEANS
    IF NOT `leave for an urgent need`
    THEN `not under s 79: the court has not given leave for an urgent need; you must first comply with chapter 5 (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `terminal condition, trial expedited` AND `priority ordered`
         THEN `yes, with the court's leave, and it is not stayed: you have a terminal condition, the trial is to be expedited and given priority; chapter 5 (other than s 79) no longer applies to the injury (s 79 (4), (5))`
         ELSE `yes, with the court's leave for an urgent need; the proceeding is stayed until you comply with chapter 5 (or it ends) (s 79 (1), (3))`

§§ `s 61 -- The respondent must try to resolve the claim; I must answer its offer`

-- s 61 (1): within 6 months after receiving a complying notice of claim (no period prescribed), the respondent must find
-- out about the accident, say whether liability is admitted or denied (and any contributory negligence as a percentage),
-- estimate the damages, and make a written offer or counteroffer, or settle by accepting the claimant's offer.
-- s 61 (4): a claimant to whom a written offer is made must respond in writing within 3 months after receiving it,
-- indicating acceptance or rejection. s 62: a court may award costs against the party that did not comply.
-- The offer is taken to be received on the day it is made (fork F7).
GIVEN `the respondent's last day serial` IS A NUMBER, `my days to answer` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Pre-court party` `Pre-court step`
`the respondent must try to resolve the claim` `the respondent's last day serial` `my days to answer` MEANS
    PARTY `the respondent`
    MUST `make a written offer of settlement or settle the claim`
    WITHIN `the respondent's last day serial`
    HENCE
        PARTY `the claimant`
        MUST `respond in writing to the offer`
        WITHIN `my days to answer`
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "no written response to the offer within 3 months: the court may award the respondent its costs caused by the noncompliance (s 61 (4), s 62 (1))"
    LEST BREACH BY `the respondent` BECAUSE "no offer or settlement within 6 months of the complying notice of claim: the court may award the claimant its costs caused by the noncompliance (s 61 (1), s 62 (2))"

DECLARE `Where the attempt to resolve my claim stands` IS ONE OF
    `the respondent has not made an offer yet, and its 6 months have not ended (s 61 (1))`
    `the respondent did not make a written offer or settle within 6 months (s 61 (1)): a court may award you costs caused by it (s 62 (2)) and may order it to comply (s 78)`
    `the respondent made an offer, and your 3 months to answer it in writing have not ended (s 61 (4))`
    `the respondent made an offer, and you answered it in writing within 3 months (s 61 (4)): both duties are met`
    `the respondent made an offer, and you did not answer it in writing within 3 months (s 61 (4)): a court may award the respondent costs caused by it (s 62 (1))`

@export Has the respondent met its duty to try to resolve my claim, and have I answered its offer in time (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 61)? The respondent has 6 months after receiving a complying notice of claim to make a written offer or settle; you then have 3 months to accept or reject the offer in writing.
GIVEN `complying notice received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the respondent received your complying notice of claim (or the day your notice was taken to be complying, s 61 (2))
      `offer made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent has made you a written offer or counteroffer of settlement (or settled by accepting your offer)
      `offer received on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day you received it (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `I answered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have answered the offer in writing, accepting or rejecting it
      `I answered on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day you did (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Where the attempt to resolve my claim stands`
`s 61 -- where does the attempt to resolve my claim stand` `complying notice received on` `offer made` `offer received on` `I answered` `I answered on` `today` MEANS
    IF NOT `offer made`
    THEN IF `the day` `today` `is after` `the respondent's last day`
         THEN `the respondent did not make a written offer or settle within 6 months (s 61 (1)): a court may award you costs caused by it (s 62 (2)) and may order it to comply (s 78)`
         ELSE `the respondent has not made an offer yet, and its 6 months have not ended (s 61 (1))`
    ELSE IF `the day` `offer received on` `is after` `the respondent's last day`
         THEN `the respondent did not make a written offer or settle within 6 months (s 61 (1)): a court may award you costs caused by it (s 62 (2)) and may order it to comply (s 78)`
         ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the respondent must try to resolve the claim` (DATE_SERIAL `the respondent's last day`) `my days to answer`) 0 `the events`
              WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the respondent made an offer, and you answered it in writing within 3 months (s 61 (4)): both duties are met`
              OTHERWISE
                  IF `I answered` OR `the day` `today` `is after` `my last day to answer`
                  THEN `the respondent made an offer, and you did not answer it in writing within 3 months (s 61 (4)): a court may award the respondent costs caused by it (s 62 (1))`
                  ELSE `the respondent made an offer, and your 3 months to answer it in writing have not ended (s 61 (4))`
    WHERE
        `the respondent's last day` MEANS `the day` 6 `months after` `complying notice received on`
        `my last day to answer` MEANS `the day` 3 `months after` `offer received on`
        `my days to answer` MEANS `the days from` `offer received on` `to` `my last day to answer`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `I answered`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the respondent` `make a written offer of settlement or settle the claim` (DATE_SERIAL `offer received on`)), (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `I answered on`)), (EVENT `the claimant` `respond in writing to the offer` (DATE_SERIAL `I answered on`))
            ELSE LIST (EVENT `the respondent` `make a written offer of settlement or settle the claim` (DATE_SERIAL `offer received on`)), (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `today`))

§§ `Deadlines in chapter 5`

-- The respondent's steps and the Act's periods where no period is prescribed (the Regulation is not deposited).
DECLARE `A step the respondent must take` IS ONE OF
    `tell me about anyone else who may be liable, and send them my notice (s 51 (9)): counted from the day it received my notice`
    `tell me about anyone else who may be liable, and send them my notice, for a motor accident claim (s 51A): counted from the day it received my notice`
    `give me its preliminary response (s 52 (1)): counted from the day it received my notice`
    `respond after I gave it the further information it asked for (s 52 (3)): counted from the day I gave the information`
    `tell me whether it is satisfied with my fix (s 54 (4)): counted from the day the period it gave me ends`
    `say whether it admits liability and make an offer or settle (s 61 (1)): counted from the day it received my complying notice`
    `respond in writing to my settlement offer (s 61 (4)): counted from the day it received the offer`
    `give me copies of its reports and documents about the accident and my condition (s 68 (2) (a)): counted from the day it received my complying notice`
    `give me a report or document that came into its possession later (s 68 (2) (b)): counted from the day it came into its possession`
    `answer my request for information about the accident (s 68 (3)): counted from the day it received the request`
    `give me copies I required, out of material it offered for inspection (s 71 (4)): counted from the day I made the requirement or, over 200 pages, the day I paid`

@export By when must the respondent take this step in the pre-court procedure for my personal injury claim (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 51, 51A, 52, 54, 61, 68, 71)? Uses the Act's own periods (1 month, 7 days, 6 months, 3 months); a period prescribed by regulation would change the answer.
GIVEN `the step` IS A `A step the respondent must take` @desc Which step of the respondent you are asking about; each says which day its period counts from
      `the day it counts from` IS A DATE @desc The day that step's period counts from, as the step describes
GIVETH A DATE
`chapter 5 -- by when must the respondent take this step` `the step` `the day it counts from` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the step`
    WHEN `tell me about anyone else who may be liable, and send them my notice (s 51 (9)): counted from the day it received my notice` THEN `the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`
    WHEN `tell me about anyone else who may be liable, and send them my notice, for a motor accident claim (s 51A): counted from the day it received my notice` THEN `the date` 7 `days after` `the day it counts from`
    WHEN `give me its preliminary response (s 52 (1)): counted from the day it received my notice` THEN `the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`
    WHEN `respond after I gave it the further information it asked for (s 52 (3)): counted from the day I gave the information` THEN `the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`
    WHEN `tell me whether it is satisfied with my fix (s 54 (4)): counted from the day the period it gave me ends` THEN `the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`
    WHEN `say whether it admits liability and make an offer or settle (s 61 (1)): counted from the day it received my complying notice` THEN `the day` 6 `months after` `the day it counts from`
    WHEN `respond in writing to my settlement offer (s 61 (4)): counted from the day it received the offer` THEN `the day` 3 `months after` `the day it counts from`
    WHEN `give me copies of its reports and documents about the accident and my condition (s 68 (2) (a)): counted from the day it received my complying notice` THEN `the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`
    WHEN `give me a report or document that came into its possession later (s 68 (2) (b)): counted from the day it came into its possession` THEN `the date` 7 `days after` `the day it counts from`
    WHEN `answer my request for information about the accident (s 68 (3)): counted from the day it received the request` THEN `the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`
    WHEN `give me copies I required, out of material it offered for inspection (s 71 (4)): counted from the day I made the requirement or, over 200 pages, the day I paid` THEN `the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`

-- The claimant's own steps. s 55 (3) (b) (telling other respondents about a later respondent) has only a period
-- "prescribed by regulation", with no period in the Act: NOTHING (the Regulation is not deposited).
DECLARE `A step I must take` IS ONE OF
    `give the respondent the further information it asked for, or insist it is properly a respondent (s 52 (2)): counted from the day it told me`
    `accept, or dispute, the respondent's statement that it is not properly a respondent (s 52 (4)): counted from the day it told me`
    `give the respondent copies of my reports and documents about the accident and my condition (s 64 (2) (a)): counted from the day I gave notice of the claim`
    `give the respondent a report or document that came into my possession later (s 64 (2) (b)): counted from the day it came into my possession`
    `answer the respondent's reasonable request for information (s 64 (3)): counted from the day I received the request`
    `respond in writing to the respondent's settlement offer (s 61 (4)): counted from the day I received the offer`
    `tell each other respondent that I added a later respondent (s 55 (3) (b)): only a period prescribed by regulation`

@export By when must I take this step in the pre-court procedure for my personal injury claim (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 52, 55, 61, 64)? Uses the Act's own periods (1 month, 7 days, 3 months); answers no date where the Act leaves the period entirely to a regulation.
GIVEN `the step` IS A `A step I must take` @desc Which of your steps you are asking about; each says which day its period counts from
      `the day it counts from` IS A DATE @desc The day that step's period counts from, as the step describes
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`chapter 5 -- by when must I take this step` `the step` `the day it counts from` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the step`
    WHEN `give the respondent the further information it asked for, or insist it is properly a respondent (s 52 (2)): counted from the day it told me` THEN JUST (`the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`)
    WHEN `accept, or dispute, the respondent's statement that it is not properly a respondent (s 52 (4)): counted from the day it told me` THEN JUST (`the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`)
    WHEN `give the respondent copies of my reports and documents about the accident and my condition (s 64 (2) (a)): counted from the day I gave notice of the claim` THEN JUST (`the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`)
    WHEN `give the respondent a report or document that came into my possession later (s 64 (2) (b)): counted from the day it came into my possession` THEN JUST (`the date` 7 `days after` `the day it counts from`)
    WHEN `answer the respondent's reasonable request for information (s 64 (3)): counted from the day I received the request` THEN JUST (`the day` 1 `months after` `the day it counts from`)
    WHEN `respond in writing to the respondent's settlement offer (s 61 (4)): counted from the day I received the offer` THEN JUST (`the day` 3 `months after` `the day it counts from`)
    WHEN `tell each other respondent that I added a later respondent (s 55 (3) (b)): only a period prescribed by regulation` THEN NOTHING

§§ `ss 66, 67 and 71 -- Expert reports, examinations and copying`

-- s 66: if an expert report is obtained by agreement between respondent and claimant and the claimant is liable for its
-- cost, the respondent must, at the claimant's request, reimburse the reasonable cost -- subject to any agreement between
-- them. The "if" failing is the text's no.
DECLARE `Must the respondent reimburse me for the expert report` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the respondent must reimburse you the reasonable cost of the report (s 66 (1))`
    `as your agreement with the respondent says: s 66 (1) is subject to it (s 66 (2))`
    `not yet: you must ask the respondent to reimburse you (s 66 (1))`
    `no: s 66 applies only to a report obtained by agreement whose cost you are liable for (the text's no)`

@export Must the respondent reimburse me for an expert report we arranged jointly (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 66)? Yes, at your request, for the reasonable cost of a report obtained by agreement that you are liable to pay for, unless your agreement with the respondent says otherwise.
GIVEN `obtained by agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the expert report was obtained by agreement between you and the respondent (s 65)
      `I am liable for its cost` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are liable for the cost of obtaining the report
      `I asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the respondent to reimburse you
      `we agreed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the respondent have agreed something about who pays for the report
GIVETH A `Must the respondent reimburse me for the expert report`
`s 66 -- must the respondent reimburse me for the expert report` `obtained by agreement` `I am liable for its cost` `I asked` `we agreed otherwise` MEANS
    IF NOT (`obtained by agreement` AND `I am liable for its cost`)
    THEN `no: s 66 applies only to a report obtained by agreement whose cost you are liable for (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `we agreed otherwise`
         THEN `as your agreement with the respondent says: s 66 (1) is subject to it (s 66 (2))`
         ELSE IF `I asked`
              THEN `yes: the respondent must reimburse you the reasonable cost of the report (s 66 (1))`
              ELSE `not yet: you must ask the respondent to reimburse you (s 66 (1))`

-- s 67: if the respondent wants an expert report but fails to get the claimant's agreement, the claimant must comply with
-- a request to undergo, at the respondent's expense, a medical examination (or capacity assessment) by a doctor (or
-- expert) the claimant selects from a panel of at least 3 the respondent nominates (2, if 3 are not available), unless
-- it is unreasonable or unnecessarily repetitious.
DECLARE `Must I undergo the respondent's examination` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you must undergo the examination or assessment, at the respondent's expense, by the doctor or expert you select from its panel (s 67 (2))`
    `no: you are not obliged, because it is unreasonable or unnecessarily repetitious (s 67 (3))`
    `no: the panel is too small: at least 3 doctors or experts, or 2 if 3 with the right qualifications are not available (the text's no, s 67 (2), (4))`
    `no: s 67 applies only where the respondent failed to get your agreement to a joint report, and asks for the examination at its own expense (the text's no)`

@export Must I undergo a medical examination or capacity assessment the respondent asks for (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 67)? Yes, if the respondent could not get your agreement to a joint expert report, pays for it, and lets you choose from a panel of at least 3 (or 2 if 3 are not available), unless it is unreasonable or unnecessarily repetitious.
GIVEN `no agreement to a joint report` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent wanted an expert report on the accident, your injury, your medical condition or your capacity, but failed to get your agreement to a jointly arranged one
      `at the respondent's expense` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the examination or assessment is to be at the respondent's expense
      `panel size` IS A NUMBER @desc How many doctors or experts the respondent nominated in its request for you to choose from
      `3 not available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether 3 doctors or experts with appropriate qualifications and experience in the field are not available for the panel
      `unreasonable or repetitious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the examination or assessment is unreasonable or unnecessarily repetitious (for example, you have just had the same one)
GIVETH A `Must I undergo the respondent's examination`
`s 67 -- must I undergo the respondent's examination` `no agreement to a joint report` `at the respondent's expense` `panel size` `3 not available` `unreasonable or repetitious` MEANS
    IF NOT (`no agreement to a joint report` AND `at the respondent's expense`)
    THEN `no: s 67 applies only where the respondent failed to get your agreement to a joint report, and asks for the examination at its own expense (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `panel size` LESS THAN `the smallest panel allowed`
         THEN `no: the panel is too small: at least 3 doctors or experts, or 2 if 3 with the right qualifications are not available (the text's no, s 67 (2), (4))`
         ELSE IF `unreasonable or repetitious`
              THEN `no: you are not obliged, because it is unreasonable or unnecessarily repetitious (s 67 (3))`
              ELSE `yes: you must undergo the examination or assessment, at the respondent's expense, by the doctor or expert you select from its panel (s 67 (2))`
    WHERE
        `the smallest panel allowed` MEANS
            IF `3 not available`
            THEN 2
            ELSE 3

-- s 71: where the copies a respondent (or contributor) must give under s 68, 69 or 70 come to more than 200 pages, it
-- need only offer a reasonable opportunity to inspect; copies then required must be given within 1 month after the
-- requirement (200 pages or fewer) or after the other person pays 50 cents for each page over 200.
@export How much must I pay the respondent for copies of its documents when they come to more than 200 pages (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 71 (4))? 50 cents for each page over 200; nothing for 200 pages or fewer. The answer is in dollars.
GIVEN `pages` IS A NUMBER @desc The total number of pages of the copies you require the respondent to give you
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 71 -- what must I pay for copies` `pages` MEANS
    IF `pages` GREATER THAN 200
    THEN (`pages` MINUS 200) TIMES 0.5
    ELSE 0
